## Python Startup Breakdown

Measured inside the container. The gap between S3 download completing and "Server started" is
the Python startup phase.

### Cold page cache (model files not in RAM)

| Phase | Time | Bottleneck |
|---|---|---|
| Top-level imports (torch, aiortc, numpy, etc.) | ~4s | EBS reading .so/.pyc files |
| `_import_musetalk()` (transformers, mmcv, DWPose) | ~4s | EBS reading packages + GPU side-effects |
| `load_all_model()` — **unet.pth is 3.2GB** | ~17s | **EBS at 125 MB/s = 25s to read 3.2GB** |
| Avatar cache load (runtime geometry) | 0.5s | NVMe/mmap — already fast |
| GPU warmup (batch 1-4 inference) | 3s | GPU compute |
| **Total** | **~29s** | |

### Warm page cache (model files already in RAM)

| Phase | Time | Bottleneck |
|---|---|---|
| Top-level imports | 1.9s | All in page cache |
| `_import_musetalk()` | 1.1s | All in page cache |
| `load_all_model()` | 6.7s | Deserialization + GPU transfer |
| Avatar cache load | 0.5s | Same |
| GPU warmup | 3s | Same |
| **Total** | **~14s** | |

### The entrypoint pre-warming race

The entrypoint runs `cat model_files > /dev/null &` in the background to warm page cache while
Python imports. Python imports take ~2s (warm) to ~8s (cold), then hits `load_all_model()`.
The 3.2GB unet.pth takes ~25s to read from EBS at 125 MB/s — the background `cat` cannot
finish before Python needs the file. **Pre-warming loses the race on cold cache.**

